# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET transactional integration job built on the Intropy
framework. It reads files from a Dapr file binding, publishes each one onto a
Dapr pub/sub topic, and processes them through a VETER send pipeline that
writes the transformed result to a second file binding.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10
- App type: Console job (no web endpoints)
- Runner: `Intropy.Framework.Hosting.RunToCompletion.RunToCompletionRunner`, hosting the
  framework's transactional integration lifecycle
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Pub/sub component: `internal-{{ .appId }}` — generated by the system host
  from the topology's minted InternalQueue, scoped to this component alone
- Topic: `hop` — the component-owned internal hop (receive publishes each
  source file for the send side to process). Invisible to the system
  topology; uniqueness comes from the pubsub scope, not the topic name
- Source port: `{{ .fromPort }}` — the named interface the component
  reads the outside world through; the system host declares it in
  `Ports.cs` and the Dapr binding uses the same name, resolved to a local
  folder in development. In production the same name resolves to the
  environment's binding (e.g. SFTP) from the deploy overlay's GitOps
  values — rebinding the port is a deployment change, never a code change
- Destination port: `{{ .toPort }}` — same, for the write side: declared
  as the To port, bound per environment behind the same port name
{{- if not .empty }}
- Idempotency Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Business Incident Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
{{- end }}

## Important files

- `Taskfile.yml` - build and test tasks (`task build`, `task test`).
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage image build on the .NET chiseled runtime
  (`docker build -t {{ .appId }} .`); the image contains only the app — the
  Dapr sidecar and components come from the deployment environment.
- `src/Program.cs` - entrypoint: builds the service provider via `Composition`
  and runs the `RunToCompletionRunner`.
- `src/Configuration/Composition.cs` - every service registration, shared by
  the entrypoint and the composition smoke test. `AddTransactionalIntegration`
  registers the whole receive side: the framework sweeps the source port
  (`SourcePort`), publishes each file to the internal hop, and deletes it
  once it is on the queue. There is no receive-side component code.
- `src/Configuration/Constants.cs` - component names, the source and destination ports, context metadata keys.
- `src/Send/` - the VETER send pipeline steps.
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/` - pipeline-step unit tests (pure xUnit; the only
  fakes are NSubstitute at the Sender's `IFileAdapter` constructor seam;
  `task test:unit`).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/` - receive-side, send-pipeline and composition
  tests against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes (`task test:integration`;
  `task coverage` reports branch coverage).

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json` (copied to the build output), overridden by
`appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables — `Composition` builds
`IConfiguration` from the binary directory, registers it in DI{{- if not .empty }}, and reads the
platform-service app ids from `PlatformServices:*`{{- end }}. Deployment overlays the file
via ConfigMap. Components that need typed options bind them themselves.

The file adapter behind each port is chosen by `Ports:{{ .fromPort }}` and `Ports:{{ .toPort }}`: `Kind` selects the Dapr
binding the port is deployed with (`Local`, `Sftp` or `AzureBlob`). `src/appsettings.Development.json`
selects `Local`, the local folder the system host resolves each port to (the host runs
every component as Development). `appsettings.json` sets no `Kind`: a deployment sets
`Ports__<port>__Kind` to match the binding it renders, and without it the component
fails at startup naming the port.

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this job runs via its system host, which
provides every Dapr component (storage bindings, pub/sub, platform services)
and carries the sample data used to seed a run. The component-level loop is
build and test:

```bash
task build
task test
```

`task test` runs both test projects (`task test:unit` / `task test:integration`
run them separately). The unit suite pins each pipeline step's own contract. The
integration suite runs against the real DI graph with only the externals faked —
`InMemoryFileAdapter` at both keyed adapter seams, `FakeEnqueueStep` registered
as the receive side's broker edge, and (non-empty mode)
`FakeIdempotencyServiceClient` / `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient` — and
asserts on fake state. `ReceiveSideIntegrationTests` runs the component's job
once, with the topic subscription stubbed through NSubstitute — the suite's only
mocking-framework usage. `task coverage` runs the integration suite
with branch coverage (Cobertura report under the integration project's
`TestResults/`).

## Development notes

- Source and destination `IFileAdapter` instances are registered (from configuration, see above) as keyed singletons under their port names
  (`Constants.SourcePort`, `Constants.DestinationPort`). Inject them
  via `[FromKeyedServices(...)]` or resolve via `GetRequiredKeyedService<IFileAdapter>(...)`.
- The send pipeline's `SendStep` is a `BusinessStep`, so a fault at the
  destination adapter surfaces as a business failure with an incident routed —
  not a technical failure the broker retries. The integration suite pins it.
- A source file that cannot be read or published stays in the source and fails
  the run; the next run picks it up again. The framework sets the file name on
  each file's context under `Constants.ContextKeyFileName`; the context metadata
  and the trace travel with the message to the send side.
{{- if .empty }}
- The pipelines are scaffolded without idempotency or business-incident wiring.
  When you implement the step bodies, also add `.WithIdempotency(client, idExtractor,
  dateExtractor)` and `.WithBusinessIncidents(client, messageIdExtractor, subjectExtractor)`
  to the send pipeline builder in `Send/ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs`, register the clients in
  `Configuration/Composition.cs` with `services.AddIdempotencyServiceClient()` and
  `services.AddBusinessIncidentServiceClient()`, and pick a stable business identifier
  and business timestamp for the idempotency extractors.
{{- else }}
- The send pipeline checks idempotency before transforming: the id is the order's
  `OrderId` and the timestamp its `OrderDate`, so a redelivered unchanged order
  cancels as a traceable no-op instead of writing twice. Keep the id/date extractors
  pointing at *business* fields — a per-delivery value (fresh GUID, wall-clock time)
  makes the check pass everything.
- Business failures (validation errors) are routed to the Business Incident
  Service, keyed on the order id.
- Impure values originate in `Deserializer` and travel via `Context.Metadata`
  (`order_id`, `processed_at`); later steps stay pure and unit-testable.
{{- end }}

